00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.320
we encounter.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:26.820
All right.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.070
So what are some things
that come to mind

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.620
when you think about a budget?

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.740
Probably not like
these lovely walls

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.330
in Frida Kahlo's courtyard.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.298
But there are a couple
of things you just

00:07:39.298 --> 00:07:40.590
want to keep in mind generally.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.260
So you want to keep in
mind categories, right,

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.440
so having a way to think
about how you organize

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:47.790
your financial information.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.370
You might organize it
according to a project,

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.810
you might organize it according
to the type of expense.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.210
So is it a salary?

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.060
Is it something physical,
like the space or the studio

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.900
you might be renting,
marketing expenses, which

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:02.620
can be tangible and intangible.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.240
So we're now in this age
of everything is online,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.310
so you're creating
digital posters

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:09.790
and all of those things.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:12.430
But you have to think
about are you using Adobe?

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.480
Are you using a Mac product?

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.590
How are you creating that?

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:17.760
What is it costing you?

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:26.420
What are you getting,
maybe in kind or for free?

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.040
What are some
things that you may

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:31.550
be able to use as an MIT
student or a student that

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.260
are available to
you now, but you

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.468
would have to think about
how are you going to budget

00:08:35.468 --> 00:08:38.150
and pay for those maybe
once you graduate,

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:41.210
or once you use up all of your
Amazon Web Services credits,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.350
or those kinds of things.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.050
So just start to
think about, as you're

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.870
building a budget, as
you're paying for things,

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:50.030
think about what are the
things that you absolutely

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:53.180
need to provide your
product or service,

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.070
and how much do they
cost, and how much

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.900
do they cost either
on an hourly basis

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:58.730
or on a per project
basis, right?

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.450
But start to think about,
you're a non-profit

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:05.390
but that doesn't mean
you're not sustainable.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.570
And I think that's really
important to recognize,

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:10.930
that non-profit
organizations have missions.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.650
But they are sustainable
organizations.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:17.650
And they just have to
think and plan differently

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:21.430
for how you match the
costs or the expenses

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.250
that you incur with
the product or service

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.770
that you're delivering
to the market.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.070
So I don't want building a
budget to be a dry topic.

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:32.290
I know it's not exciting.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.140
I know that sometimes it's
something we don't want to do.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.480
But I want you to
think of your budget

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.870
as a tool that supports
your mission and your work,

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:43.330
and not something that's
just another chore.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:46.517
There are lots of
things that are chores.

00:09:46.517 --> 00:09:48.100
But it's really
something that's going

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.310
to enable you to scale and
grow your impact in what you're

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:51.810
doing.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:58.770
All right, so building
a budget, this

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.860
is going to be the first step in
creating an accurate financial

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.560
picture of your organization.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.210
If you start to
build a budget, you

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.310
will find that it's
much easier to source

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.250
your other financial
statements, so

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.140
your income statement
or a balance sheet

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.780
once you start to have those
numbers, because then you

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.020
have an idea of how you're
allocating your resources,

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.180
and also what resources
you need to scale and grow.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.430
And you're going to find
things will change over time.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.940
You're going to see
where you have recurring

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.480
expenses that you could reduce.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.460
So you might be renting
something, and, after a while,

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.210
you realize it makes
sense to purchase it.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.945
Or you may realize that
a piece of equipment

00:10:35.945 --> 00:10:38.070
becomes outdated quickly,
and it doesn't make sense

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.600
to purchase that
piece of equipment.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:43.350
It actually makes sense
to rent it long term

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.050
or find a leasing model or
make sure you get a maintenance

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:49.020
program, because that's where
the real costs build up, right?

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.860
So information and
knowledge actually

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.150
become really critical tools in
making you a more sophisticated

00:10:57.150 --> 00:10:59.370
entrepreneur, in
terms of how you're

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.040
maximizing your resources.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.540
There's actually an
artisan, she knits hats.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.740
She lives in Vermont.

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:09.270
Her name's Hannah.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:12.360
And she has a blog called, Why
are these hats so expensive?

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.210
And she actually breaks down,
right, she takes the hat

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.670
and then she kind of takes
it apart and works backwards.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.920
And she says, this is how
much yarn goes into the hat.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.570
This is how much wool
I have to purchase.

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:24.210
This is how much dye I need.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:27.450
And she really goes through all
of those unit economics costs,

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.990
and then says this is how I come
up with the price of the hat.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:32.550
One thing that's
really important

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:34.860
is that she values her time.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.920
So she's an artisan,
but she also

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.423
says this is actually
what it costs me.

00:11:40.423 --> 00:11:42.090
This is what my time
is worth, and these

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.250
are what my living
expenses are, and she

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:47.080
factors all of those things
into the value of her time.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:50.940
So I think that's one thing,
it's very easy, especially

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.000
for anyone who's a
student full-time,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.820
to think of this as
a hobby or a project.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.680
But you really want to
think of this, again,

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.300
going back to being a
sustainable organization,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:04.240
your people are going to be
your most important assets,

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:07.120
including the founders,
especially the founders.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.630
And so you want to make
sure that you're accurately

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.010
valuing your time.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:14.380
And we'll talk about some
different ways to do that.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:16.845
But that's one area I
think where people often

00:12:16.845 --> 00:12:19.893
mis-underestimate
their expenses, right?

00:12:19.893 --> 00:12:21.060
They don't value their time.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.120
And then also as
you start to think

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.200
about what you
want to accomplish,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:32.700
what you are
accomplishing, and what

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.920
you have to change to
get there, a budget

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.710
will actually help you realize,
OK, this isn't what we need.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:40.800
Or you'll be able to
recognize seasonal patterns

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.740
in your income, and
how do you match

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.010
all of those things over time.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.910
All right.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.280
So the key, a
budget is, there are

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:56.520
going to be two sides, your
expenses and your income.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.370
So, as I mentioned,
it's really important

00:12:58.370 --> 00:13:00.770
to think about
everything that you

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.120
incur to produce your
product or service.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.500
If you have a loom, that's a
recurring fixed expense, right?

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.900
So it doesn't
matter if you weave

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.130
10 rugs or 20 rugs in a month,
you've purchased the loom

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:16.245
and that's a sunk cost.

00:13:16.245 --> 00:13:18.120
And then you're going
to have variable costs.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.500
So if you're purchasing,
if you're making more rugs,

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:26.850
you're going to incur other
materials, the thread, the dye,

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.100
all of those different things.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.820
Thinking about, if
you have anything

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:38.170
that changes seasonally in
terms of inputs or availability,

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:41.740
demand, especially I think
space in Kendall Square

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.590
is a huge one, right?

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.813
There's a seasonality to that.

00:13:46.813 --> 00:13:49.480
So those are just some different
things you want to think about.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.370
So I think really
understanding what

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:55.370
you're producing from
the beginning to the end,

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:59.800
and how that factors into
your next either sales cycle

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.240
or production cycle, or
whatever you're doing.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.660
So I don't know if anyone here
is familiar with HubSpot's

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.400
flywheel.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.070
So they talk about
the cycle of you're

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:13.780
going to basically attract
delight and then re-engage

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:15.040
your customer, right?

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.650
So you attract them,
you bring them in.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:18.460
You delight them.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:20.770
So they're like getting a
rug and then they discover,

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:23.650
Oh, there's something, when I
look at it from the other side

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:25.210
I see a completely
different pattern,

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.700
or the light looks different
in the morning than it does

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:28.700
in the afternoon, right?

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.470
And they're delighted
by these features.

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:32.470
And then you find ways
to re-engage them, right?

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.410
And so that's kind of the cycle
that you want to look for.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.120
And you want to think
about that, again,

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.920
as you start to
build your budget.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:41.260
What does that look like?

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:45.500
I mean, think about a lot of
other things that people buy,

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:49.570
so shoes and clothing and meals.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.520
The things you buy on
Saturday when you go out

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:53.228
with your friends are
different from what

00:14:53.228 --> 00:14:55.880
you get on Tuesday at lunch
when you're just in a hurry.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.213
So you want to think about
all of these different things

00:14:58.213 --> 00:15:00.325
and how they factor
into your expenses.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:04.717
All right.

00:15:04.717 --> 00:15:06.550
So as I mentioned, when
you're starting out,

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:09.040
I think that the easiest way
to think about your expenses

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.230
is in terms of categories.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.650
And you can Google these things.

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.650
I mean this is a
pretty good list

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:19.480
of the different categories.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:22.670
Workspace is often a big
one, administrative costs.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:25.840
So those are all the things
you need to keep going.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.750
G-Suite has a lot of free
things for non-profits,

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:31.000
hopefully you know that.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:34.780
Web hosting, the same, you
can find varying degrees.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.270
Things like snacks, if you're
buying snacks for your staff,

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:37.770
right?

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:40.000
I mean that's really common
in a lot of workspaces.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.450
But these are small things
that people forget, especially

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:48.700
as they start to grow or think
about leaving the MIT nest.

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:52.180
But these are the things
that are actually important

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.790
and they add up.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:58.090
Staff compensation, this is
one we talked about often.

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:01.270
You're a startup and maybe you
can't compensate people yet.

00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:04.390
But find a way to approximate
the value of the time, right?

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:08.410
So if you have someone
who's in a managerial role,

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.950
then find out what a manager
in your same market is paid.

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:15.700
I mean you can do that
on an annual salary

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:18.700
and do all the math and
break down the hours, right?

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:21.610
But those are really good ways
to budget for your expense.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.080
And then we'll talk
about recording

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:28.660
that back in, tracking that
as time that's donated.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:34.580
And then we also talked
about, in some cases,

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:37.730
it makes sense to think about
program and project expenses.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:41.320
So and this is common in
especially non-profits that

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:44.890
might have programs that are
funded by multiple donors,

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:45.390
right?

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.160
So you have to start to allocate
expenses to different programs.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.020
And then you have to decide
50% of someone's salary

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:55.570
is coming from this
funder and 50% is coming

00:16:55.570 --> 00:16:57.930
from another funder, right?

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.730
Or you may have
funders who say, I'm

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:05.550
really interested
in visual arts,

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.111
and you may have
others who say, I'm

00:17:07.111 --> 00:17:08.819
really interested in
musical arts, right?

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:13.089
And you have to find ways
to make those things work.

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:16.589
And I think tracking these
expenses, especially expenses

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.089
that might be shared across
projects, is really important.

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:20.730
So once you have
your budget, you

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:24.480
can start to figure that
out, because the funders

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:26.339
for non-profits,
especially, are going

00:17:26.339 --> 00:17:29.520
to be interested in
reporting and wanting to know

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.020
the impact of their dollars.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.480
So.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:40.670
yeah, just remember, staff
time is incredibly valuable

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.980
and don't discount
that, especially

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:45.320
as you're in a startup
kind of mode, right?

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.190
Any other comments,
thoughts, and these

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:55.820
are just some big
categories, but you

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:59.480
might find other categories
are better for your industry.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.700
I think I really like
the way that you're

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.940
talking about really making
sure that you're cognizant,

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.810
even if you don't have
the financial outlay

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.595
capacities to pay
people for their time,

00:18:10.595 --> 00:18:13.220
you've got to figure out like in
a perfect world, how much does

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:14.690
it cost to run this thing.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:20.150
So you cannot over emphasize
enough the importance

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:24.620
of accounting for the snacks,
accounting for the unpaid labor

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:27.830
that should ideally turn into
paid labor when there's capital

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:28.350
for that.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:29.450
So thank you for that.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:33.005
All right.

00:18:33.005 --> 00:18:36.560
So we go on.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.650
All right, income.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:44.107
So what do people think of when
they think of income, right?

00:18:44.107 --> 00:18:45.440
It's money that comes in, right?

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.097
And this gets tricky.

00:18:47.097 --> 00:18:48.680
So there's income,
which is everything

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:49.888
that comes into your venture.

00:18:49.888 --> 00:18:54.140
So that's prize money,
grant funds, that volunteer,

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:58.190
that start-up, that
sweat equity, right?

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:01.340
Anything else that might
be donated, but also

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:04.050
think about what are the costs
of accessing those, right?

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:05.900
So you can enter
into prizes, you

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:09.112
can enter into competitions, you
can spend time writing grants,

00:19:09.112 --> 00:19:10.820
but there are costs
associated with that.

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:11.940
And again it's time.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:16.520
It's what you need to produce
high quality visual materials,

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:17.870
if you have to produce videos.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:22.250
Do you have to buy those
little cameras for your iPhone?

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:25.850
All of those things, all are
part of your equipment costs

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:27.710
and your production
costs, right?

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:30.440
So there's income.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:31.230
There's revenue.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.140
So that's usually what
comes in from sales

00:19:33.140 --> 00:19:35.870
or from contracts, if
you're selling tickets,

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:40.260
if you're selling art,
whatever it may be.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:46.390
And then profit is
your income, sorry,

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:48.980
profit is your revenue
minus all your costs.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:51.700
And then what's left over
is hopefully your profit.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:56.840
This gets tricky with
non-profits, right?

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:58.760
Ideally you spent all
the money and that's

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.010
what funders and donors
want to see, right?

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:02.510
If you have money
left over, they're

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:03.987
like, why is there
money left over?

00:20:03.987 --> 00:20:05.570
We're not going to
give you more money

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:08.720
until you spend the money
we've already given you, so.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:14.210
So we'll also go over an
example that deals with that.

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:16.820
But, again, think about
all the different, there's

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.350
all the money that comes in.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.800
And it's really important to
track that, because, as I said,

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:25.860
it helps you understand your
sources and uses of funds.

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.530
It also helps you understand,
how are you actually allocating

00:20:30.530 --> 00:20:33.260
that money, and how can
you show impact, right?

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:36.260
So I think different
sources of income

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:40.100
usually have different,
not usually, but sometimes,

00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:42.920
have different impact
metrics requirements, right?

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:45.500
So understanding all
of those things, as you

00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:48.260
go into the process,
and also thinking about,

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.240
you can work with large funders.

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:52.490
But what are their requirements?

00:20:52.490 --> 00:20:53.990
Do they want
quarterly reporting?

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:55.610
Do they want
semiannual reporting?

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.440
Do they want annual
reporting, right?

00:20:57.440 --> 00:20:59.510
And all of those
things usually add up

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:02.060
to someone's time on staff.

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:03.530
So if you're a
startup, it's going

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:08.420
to be the entrepreneur's
time and/or maybe

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:11.540
if you're lucky enough to
have a UROP or an intern

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:13.430
or someone like that.

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:16.700
But thinking about all of
those different requirements

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:20.220
and pieces, the other thing
to think about with income,

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:23.000
especially donations
or grants, sometimes

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.870
some large organizations also
have attribution requirements.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:31.040
So they may want to take
credit in terms of their impact

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:33.290
and their reporting for
the things you're doing.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.450
And it's a great way
to get recognition.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:37.910
It's a great way to
scale but it's also

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:40.910
kind of thinking about
what direction you

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:43.070
want your organization
or your venture to go.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.260
All right, any questions?

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.880
One other thing to mention,
depending on where you are

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.370
or the organization
granting prize money,

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.140
sometimes it can be taxable.

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:04.390
So just especially if you're
an international student,

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:08.860
just beware of any
potential taxation.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.250
Some organizations give
checks or give prize money

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.770
to the individual who
entered the competition.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:17.390
Others might award it
to the organization.

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:20.350
So just be aware
of those things.

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.090
All right.

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:29.050
So many years ago, when
I was a graduate student,

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:32.860
I worked for the lending
organization in Oaxaca, Mexico.

00:22:32.860 --> 00:22:36.670
And they had an
ecotourism model and they

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:43.810
used that to educate visitors
to Oaxaca about local arts

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:46.390
and the local economy
and the local culture.

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:48.280
And then they also
provided loans

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:52.030
to micro and small
enterprises, most of which

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:56.920
were run by women, either
in the arts, in agriculture,

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:58.900
or running small
businesses like restaurants

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:01.670
and providing services.

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:05.770
So this is sort of what I just
did a very rough budget of what

00:23:05.770 --> 00:23:08.020
their operations
looked like, and then

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:12.040
kind of what they did, how
they met their ultimate goal

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:14.590
of supplying loans to artisans.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:18.290
So these were their three
main sources of income.

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:21.010
So tours, so these
were visits out

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:23.440
to the villages,
donations, so people

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:26.170
who just liked the
organization, and then

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:28.035
they also did some
educational courses.

00:23:28.035 --> 00:23:30.160
So you could come and you
could do a cooking course

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:31.453
in the village.

00:23:31.453 --> 00:23:36.940
It was a special
day-long activity

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:39.250
And they had two
people on staff.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.020
They had lots of volunteers.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:43.960
So they had university students
who got volunteer credit

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:48.502
for building out their website,
or for creating a new logo

00:23:48.502 --> 00:23:49.460
and that kind of thing.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:52.520
So they made the most
of those resources.

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:56.530
But these were their actual
staff salary expenditures.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:24:00.730
Programs for tourists, so
there was transportation cost

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:04.090
that was pretty much a
fixed cost every month.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:06.580
And then lunches, which varied
depending on how many people

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:08.060
are on the tour.

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:10.570
And then for courses,
same thing, same fixed

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:12.970
transportation
cost, lunches varied

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:16.460
depending on participation,
and the materials.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.060
So like I said,
if you're cooking

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.290
that's all of the ingredients.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:23.350
If someone's doing weaving,
it's all of the yarn,

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:24.317
and that kind of thing.

00:24:24.317 --> 00:24:25.900
And then special
events, so they would

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:29.760
host dinners, small concerts.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:34.120
Their administration costs,
so marketing was pretty low.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:40.910
They would just do Facebook
ads, but not too bad, web site,

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:45.580
and then they did staff lunches
for the volunteers every week.

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:50.350
And then whatever was left over,
so all the sources of income,

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.780
plus all the expenses
went into this loans

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:54.580
to artisans category, right?

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:58.990
So we talked about non-profit
can be keeping profit

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:01.340
and retained earnings
and all that stuff.

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:04.180
So at the end of the month,
whatever was left over

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:07.120
got converted into loan money.

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.628
Because they were non-profit
and not a lending institution,

00:25:09.628 --> 00:25:10.420
they could do this.

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:12.430
And so the loans were
basically, if they

00:25:12.430 --> 00:25:15.550
got repaid like it
was great, but, you

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:20.740
know, they were not under the
same requirements as a bank

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.780
or a financial institution,

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:25.210
Could we do a quick
talk about that, just

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:26.680
a really glancing overview.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:30.820
I think non-profit can often be
perceived almost as a misnomer.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.760
And, like you were saying,
loans to artisans, things

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:38.740
like that, if you have any
excess income over expenses,

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:41.320
you're allowed to allocate that
pretty much as long as it's

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:46.150
in alignment with the stated
mission of the organization.

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:46.650
Yeah.

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:48.360
Yeah, thank you for
clarifying that.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:52.080
Yeah, so that's kind of what
goes back to your non-profit

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:53.610
has a mission, right?

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.830
And that's why having
these categories

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:57.870
in these budget
items, so you can

00:25:57.870 --> 00:26:02.220
see programs that are offered,
so tours and courses are broken

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:02.730
out, right?

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:05.010
And then administration
applies to all of these.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:06.870
The executive director
and administrator

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:09.660
work for both of these programs.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:13.050
And then, at the end, like,
yes, the loans are an expense.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.640
They don't
necessarily come back.

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:18.360
But all of these activities
support this core mission.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.560
And just in some way you
can see different people

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:26.970
maybe outside of this
world would say, Oh, well,

00:26:26.970 --> 00:26:29.880
those are just
basically charity.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:35.980
And like, yes and no, and that's
more of a philosophical debate.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:40.740
But I think the point is
that there was clarity.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.627
There's transparency in what
the organization was doing.

00:26:43.627 --> 00:26:45.460
So all of those things
are really important.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:49.200
This was a 501 (3) (c)
registered in the United States

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:50.580
with operations in Mexico.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:56.070
So you have the ability to
collect these funds from donors

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:59.550
in the US and then
administer them

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:02.590
in accord with the stated
mission and purpose.

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:03.990
So, yeah.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:08.260
So, I mean, there are lots
of different ways to do this.

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:10.800
You may not be doing
that, or you may not

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:15.270
be facilitating a
lending program.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.210
But just thinking about,
that's really the purpose

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:24.420
is you're creating value and
how are you sharing that value.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:28.440
So, yeah, and I worked in a
hybrid organization as well.

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:31.880
So if anybody does have a
hybrid they're thinking about,

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:36.480
we can talk more
about that offline.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:39.280
But, yeah, there are a lot
of different ways to do this.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:47.730
So just a couple of things,
one thing I think that applies,

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:49.590
and this goes back to
sustainability very

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:52.740
much to for-profit and
non-profit startups,

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:54.660
is that, at the
beginning, you will

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:59.040
be maximizing the talents
of the individuals who

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.380
work in your
organization, to really

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:03.027
be playing to their strengths.

00:28:03.027 --> 00:28:04.860
As your business starts
to grow, then you'll

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:07.770
find you need to look
for individuals who

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:09.820
have more specific strengths.

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:13.380
Usually you're looking for
more senior managers, or maybe

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:16.260
a highly specialized skill set.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.660
And so just thinking
about, as you

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:21.190
start to allocate those expenses
and those salaries, right,

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:23.540
so you may have someone
who does something,

00:28:23.540 --> 00:28:26.478
that teaches a very
specific course,

00:28:26.478 --> 00:28:28.020
or is an expert in
something that has

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:32.130
to do with tours or marketing.

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:34.020
So again, just thinking
about, but once you

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.670
have these categories
you can then

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:40.980
make a decision about how
the people spend their time

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:43.200
or how equipment and
different resources

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:45.960
are used in the organization.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.420
Income is almost always the
easiest part to figure out.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:51.150
You have money
coming in, hopefully

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.672
it's going into one
of these categories.

00:28:53.672 --> 00:28:55.380
This stuff can get a
little bit trickier,

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:58.825
and as your organization grows
the categories may change.

00:28:58.825 --> 00:29:01.200
So this particular organization
looked at transportation.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:03.100
And after a time
they realized, hey,

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:06.510
we're actually better off if
we buy our own van, right?

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:08.400
This is a recurring
expense, and there

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.860
are a lot of things
that we could

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:15.660
do if we didn't have this lump
sum every month going out.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:17.590
So those are things
that are really useful.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:20.880
The other thing is to recognize
there's seasonality, right?

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:25.188
So down here they are a
really lean organization,

00:29:25.188 --> 00:29:27.480
and they could just make
loans to artisans every month.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:29.700
But you might have a
month that's negative,

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.477
and the next month is positive.

00:29:31.477 --> 00:29:33.810
And then you balance that out
at the end of the quarter,

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:39.428
or the end of the year, or
whatever your tax situation is.

00:29:39.428 --> 00:29:40.720
And there's seasonality, right?

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:42.790
July and August you
have a lot of tourists.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:44.740
Or July you have
a lot, August you

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:47.110
have a lot in the beginning,
not so many in the end,

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:50.500
and then in September, you have
a bunch of retired teachers

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.273
who love to travel.

00:29:52.273 --> 00:29:54.190
In September, I mean,
that was the seasonality

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:55.190
of this business, right?

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:58.350
So we always know we'll
get a bump up in September.

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.790
All right, any
questions at this point?

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.530
All right.

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:17.170
One thing is, especially
if you're a startup,

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:20.380
and this applies to
for-profit and non-profit,

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:22.570
if you have early
clients or people who

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.660
are interested in what you're
doing, ask them to fund this.

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:28.070
You don't have to say, will
you fund my first project.

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:29.740
But you can say,
we need a deposit,

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:31.780
or we need 50% upfront, right?

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:35.680
So figure out what your
costs are or what you need.

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:37.913
And maybe you really
need that Adobe license.

00:30:37.913 --> 00:30:39.580
And you shouldn't
right now, because you

00:30:39.580 --> 00:30:41.080
can get it for free from MIT.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.060
But maybe you really
need something.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:43.880
You need a camera.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.870
You need a technology license.

00:30:46.870 --> 00:30:48.760
And you don't have
to say to the client,

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:51.370
will you give me the money
to buy this technology,

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:53.920
or I need the money upfront
for this technology license.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:57.040
But you can use your budget
and you can use your expenses,

00:30:57.040 --> 00:31:00.700
and you can say, OK, this is
a fair deposit price, right?

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:02.290
We're asking for 50% upfront.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:07.150
But again having a
budget is a good way,

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:09.220
it's also great when
you start to fundraise.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:14.070
If you need $1,000 and someone
says, we can give you $500,

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:15.180
what can you do with that?

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:16.930
Then you can say, OK,
these are the things

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:19.097
that are nice to have, and
then these are the things

00:31:19.097 --> 00:31:22.060
that we absolutely need,
to be able to move forward

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:23.980
and produce this, right?

00:31:23.980 --> 00:31:26.830
But having that knowledge
and information,

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:28.900
and being able to put
those project budget

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:32.890
expenses in an appendix if
somebody asks to see them,

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:34.390
definitely gives
you an advantage.

00:31:38.190 --> 00:31:38.775
All right.

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:44.040
So we can spend a
little bit of time,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:48.300
so fundraising, obviously,
MIT is a fantastic environment

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:51.150
for grants and prizes,
anything that's

00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:55.770
non-dilutive or isn't incurring
any debt is fantastic.

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:57.570
And also use that,
this is a great time

00:31:57.570 --> 00:31:59.610
to focus on the
sustainability of your models.

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:03.060
So any non-profit that's
successful long term

00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:07.020
has some kind of sustainability
into their operations,

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:10.970
and has clients who find value
in what they're doing, right?

00:32:13.490 --> 00:32:16.325
Always evaluate whether your
mission aligns with that

00:32:16.325 --> 00:32:17.450
of your funders and donors.

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:21.710
We're at a time where
it's really apparent why

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:24.470
we need to do this, also
at a time where it's

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:25.890
easier than ever to do this.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:29.660
So there are a lot of tools
out there to evaluate.

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:34.490
People are pretty honest now
through all kinds of mediums.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:37.730
But just make sure that what
you are doing is aligned

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:40.370
and that you're not going
to be experiencing mission

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:44.270
drift or things like that.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:46.460
Consider reporting
requirements and attribution.

00:32:46.460 --> 00:32:49.460
So I've heard from different
non-profits, maybe not

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:51.890
necessarily in the arts,
that sometimes getting

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:56.150
a little bit of money from
a really big name donor can

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:57.810
actually be very burdensome.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:02.330
So really think about what the
value of that relationship is,

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:06.080
beyond just a
donation or grants.

00:33:06.080 --> 00:33:09.170
Make sure, especially with
grants now, some of them

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:11.480
can be recoverable grants.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:15.655
So those that would be income
coming into your budget,

00:33:15.655 --> 00:33:17.030
that would also
be something that

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:20.640
lies on your balance sheet
and your financial statement.

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:23.450
So if you take
off in five years,

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:26.300
some organizations can
say, hey, remember us?

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:28.760
We gave you your
first big grant.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:30.530
So just keep those
things in mind.

00:33:30.530 --> 00:33:33.020
They're great.

00:33:33.020 --> 00:33:36.890
But as you start to track
all of your sources of income

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:40.520
and your expenses, don't
lose sight of that.

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:45.560
One thing is, this is tricky
with measuring and evaluating

00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:47.450
the impact of what you're doing.

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:49.730
You should, especially
at the early stages,

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:52.190
have some kind of key
metrics that you're

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:54.350
tracking and defining, right?

00:33:54.350 --> 00:33:57.590
So that's one thing that
really sets non-profits apart,

00:33:57.590 --> 00:33:59.880
and for-profit
ventures do it as well.

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:03.740
But I think you can really,
if you can demonstrate

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:05.540
the impact of what
you're doing, and if you

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:08.900
have good metrics that make
sense, funders and donors will

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:10.870
be more likely to work with you.

00:34:10.870 --> 00:34:13.940
There are lists, so you can go
to the global impact investing

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:14.510
network.

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:16.718
There are all kinds of lists
and frameworks out there

00:34:16.718 --> 00:34:19.040
for impact.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.290
But just make sure
that you're choosing

00:34:21.290 --> 00:34:23.270
what makes sense for you.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.650
And I think it's OK to start
with some basic things.

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.250
And then you can start to
think about things that

00:34:28.250 --> 00:34:29.540
might be more of a stretch.

00:34:29.540 --> 00:34:31.340
So maybe they're difficult
to measure right now,

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:32.382
or you're not doing them.

00:34:32.382 --> 00:34:33.983
But you can also
use those to set

00:34:33.983 --> 00:34:35.150
goals for your organization.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:36.810
Shannon?

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.610
I think that's an
incredibly good point.

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:41.760
A lot of the value added,
I think, of the 15K,

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:43.980
compared to some of
the other contests,

00:34:43.980 --> 00:34:48.480
is that we do a sort of emphasis
on storytelling and narrative

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:52.469
and qualitative points,
because a lot of the arts,

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.679
it can be a little trickier
to nail down some quant data,

00:34:55.679 --> 00:34:58.920
but quant data benchmarks,
impact evaluation,

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:01.920
are incredibly important
for ongoing viability

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:03.100
and sustainability.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.700
So even if you're
perfecting your pitch

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:07.200
and perfecting your
talking points,

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:10.188
you've also still got to be
able to show that it's working.

00:35:10.188 --> 00:35:11.730
And you need to know
how you're going

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:15.240
to measure that it's working,
so incredibly important.

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:21.510
That and along getting any
kind of client testimonial

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:24.390
is really, really important.

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:26.460
Yeah, so anything
you can do to have

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:29.820
that kind of qualitative feature
as well as that quantitative

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:31.950
is great.

00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:35.610
There's a journalist, she
was a journalist in residence

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:36.630
with the Legatum Center.

00:35:36.630 --> 00:35:40.620
Unfortunately COVID hit
and she had to go home

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.470
to Alexandria, Virginia.

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:46.680
But she does write a lot
about local entrepreneurs

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:48.150
all over the US.

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:52.230
She has a website called
Times of Entrepreneurship.

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:57.390
And so she also lists local
resources for entrepreneurs.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:00.900
When I say local, I mean in
different regions of the US.

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:02.090
But she's a great person.

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:03.840
And if you're doing
something really cool,

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:06.360
it's worth following her
and like raising your hand

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:08.910
on Twitter or whatever,
and saying, hey, I'm

00:36:08.910 --> 00:36:12.210
doing this cool thing that's
providing jobs for artisans

00:36:12.210 --> 00:36:14.610
in the US, or
creating a platform,

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.760
or getting recognition for
what they're doing, right?

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:23.410
So also I think we're in a time
now where everything's online.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:25.890
But I've also seen people
make really great connections,

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:29.160
whether to funders or
donors or just key talent,

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:31.710
by using those
different channels that

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:33.640
are available to you.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:36.230
I think that the adage
of you ask for advice

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:38.730
and you get money, and you ask
for money and you get advice,

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:44.470
probably applies
across the board.

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:48.900
So a few other things
on fundraising,

00:36:48.900 --> 00:36:51.930
think about if you
have co-founders,

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:55.410
think about your
relationship with them,

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:57.930
how everyone is
contributing to the team,

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:00.540
and what each team
member's goals are.

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:02.820
So that's really
important as you

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:07.200
start to think about growing
or scaling or what's next.

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:09.600
The structure, so right now
you might be a non-profit,

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:12.600
but in five years it might
make sense to be a hybrid.

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:15.360
Or you might find
that, and this happened

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.950
when I was at Root
Capital, what's

00:37:16.950 --> 00:37:19.200
considered a non-profit
operation in the US

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.480
is considered a for-profit
operation somewhere else.

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:24.690
So being prepared
to adapt and, again,

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:28.680
that goes back to what is your
mission, what is your goal,

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:31.350
and who's either funding
you, investing you,

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:34.020
or supporting you.

00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:38.300
Yeah, so I think
that's about it.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:40.580
Any questions, any
thoughts on fundraising?

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:46.620
All right.

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:48.570
Good luck.

00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:51.390
So feel free to reach
out with questions.

00:37:51.390 --> 00:37:53.580
This is a general,
high-level overview.

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:56.430
But if anyone does have
more specific questions

00:37:56.430 --> 00:37:58.110
or would like to
talk through anything

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:01.140
or additional resources,
I'm fine if you

00:38:01.140 --> 00:38:03.330
want to reach out via email.

00:38:03.330 --> 00:38:04.410
Awesome.

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:07.430
We have a couple of questions,
if you don't mind some Q&amp;A now.

00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:08.055
Does that work?

00:38:08.055 --> 00:38:08.555
Yeah.

00:38:08.555 --> 00:38:09.870
That works.

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:14.610
So you hit on something
about budgeting

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:16.645
for operating,
budgeting for expenses.

00:38:16.645 --> 00:38:18.270
And I was sort of
thinking that there's

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:19.950
a lot of baggage
surrounding the sort

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:21.690
of lean non-profit mentality.

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:24.540
And that really negatively
impacts specifically arts

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:26.220
driven non-profit orgs.

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:30.090
So like how, is there
any more specificity

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:32.400
you can give about
recommending budgeting

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:34.740
for general ops
and other expenses,

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:36.990
in case you have
donors who strongly

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:39.720
prefer that their funds
be restricted exclusively

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:41.280
to program use?

00:38:41.280 --> 00:38:43.440
Yeah, that's really challenging.

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:47.175
And I think one of the things
that can help entrepreneurs

00:38:47.175 --> 00:38:51.870
with that is going back to
this impact metrics, right?

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:54.630
So if you show that,
to deliver a program,

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:57.240
you have to have space, right?

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:00.660
You can't, well,
hopefully, we're

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:03.890
returning to a
non-COVID world, right?

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:06.030
So right now like
renting an office space

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:07.770
may be hard to justify.

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:09.930
But if you're thinking
like we actually

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:15.870
need a space, because we
have a program and people,

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:18.280
for example, need to
be close to a T, right?

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:21.090
So if you're running an
arts program for children

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:22.870
after school, they
need to be close.

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:25.370
They probably need to be close
to a certain location, right?

00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:27.030
There's a convenience
element there.

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:33.300
And I think having understanding
for whom you're providing

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:36.440
services and what are the
true costs that go into that,

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:39.240
and the value of what
you're doing, can help.

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:45.830
And some funders might, I want
to say, dig in their heels.

00:39:45.830 --> 00:39:47.210
They might be more resistant.

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:50.790
One thing to be aware of is that
sometimes large organizations,

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:54.140
especially, have one set
of grant requirements.

00:39:54.140 --> 00:39:57.590
And they will just make
all of the requirements

00:39:57.590 --> 00:40:03.500
the same for all of their
receiving organizations.

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:07.700
And I think that's where having
a really good program officer,

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:09.570
having a good
relationship is key.

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:12.470
The other thing, too, is
there are some funders who

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:13.160
understand that.

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:15.740
And they're willing
to fund those things.

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:18.620
And I think being savvy
in your fundraising

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:21.650
and finding several
good partners,

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:23.210
and some who it's
like, hey, we have

00:40:23.210 --> 00:40:27.500
these operational expenses,
and we have to cover them.

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:29.660
In terms of your
reporting, you know

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:34.580
you have to record your
expenses on your books, right?

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:37.790
So every time you have to
buy posterboard or paperclips

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:41.180
or copier paper,
all of those things

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:44.090
are line items on your books.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:47.630
But in terms of your
administrative expenses,

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:52.993
you can also use
categories to say,

00:40:52.993 --> 00:40:54.660
these are the things
we're doing, right?

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.000
So you don't have
funders who are

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:57.542
looking at like
every time you bought

00:40:57.542 --> 00:40:58.795
Kind bars for the office.

00:40:58.795 --> 00:40:59.420
But it's like--

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:03.080
You know, 'cause the
lights need to stay on,

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:06.380
you need toilet paper
in the bathroom.

00:41:06.380 --> 00:41:07.820
Right.

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.772
But I think if you
can also find ways,

00:41:09.772 --> 00:41:12.230
and this is where you go back
to allocating those expenses,

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:14.490
right?

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:17.760
If you have multiple
donors or funders,

00:41:17.760 --> 00:41:21.090
it's like, well, you know,
30% of our electric bill

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:24.120
went to this program,
because we ran classes

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:28.680
for three days a week
for a whole semester.

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:31.810
And that's what you
need to do that.

00:41:31.810 --> 00:41:37.780
So relationships are tricky.

00:41:37.780 --> 00:41:38.780
But you can manage them.

00:41:38.780 --> 00:41:42.420
And when I've seen those kinds
of things managed successfully,

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:48.610
it's because everyone has
very good information,

00:41:48.610 --> 00:41:51.160
good information that allows
them to demonstrate that,

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:55.600
yes, we needed these kinds of
operational overhead things,

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:58.270
but this is how they directly
contributed to the program.

00:41:58.270 --> 00:42:00.640
And I think saying we're
not charging 100% of this

00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:02.930
to this program, right, if
you have the flexibility

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:03.430
to do that.

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:08.050
We're charging this 30%, because
the program you're funding

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:11.510
is 30% of all of our operations.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:12.250
Perfect.

00:42:12.250 --> 00:42:15.230
Thank you.

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:19.530
One piece of insight that may
be helpful for students watching

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:22.200
this asynchronously
or students now.

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:24.480
What do you see as the
most common mistake

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:28.230
that first time founders,
students, typically

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:30.570
make with budgets, and
what would your advice

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:33.450
be to correct that
or get ahead of that?

00:42:33.450 --> 00:42:36.570
I think one thing I see, and
I talked to this already,

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:38.850
is people not
valuing their time.

00:42:42.250 --> 00:42:45.040
And so I think we talked about
different ways to benchmark

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:45.540
that.

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:52.920
So making sure that that's been
accounted for somehow, as you

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.980
look forward to what
it would actually

00:42:54.980 --> 00:42:58.350
cost to run your business,
or to run your organization

00:42:58.350 --> 00:42:59.790
as a business.

00:42:59.790 --> 00:43:02.820
I think the other thing,
too, is just all the smaller

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:03.950
things add up, right?

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:07.090
And you just don't
think about it.

00:43:07.090 --> 00:43:12.180
And so I would say really just
tracking all of the expenses

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:15.520
and all of the things that
you need to do in a day.

00:43:15.520 --> 00:43:19.330
So as students you have tons
of resources available to you.

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:22.530
But as I said, and I've seen
this when students graduate,

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:25.320
it's the Adobe
license that you need.

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:29.880
Or it's you're no longer at
MIT and so if something happens

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:33.930
to your computer, you can't
just go to IS&amp;T and drop it off,

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:34.440
right?

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:35.690
You know, that's a real thing.

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:42.390
So knowing like I need a $2,500
computer to create this design

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:44.370
or to do my work or
to have the editing

00:43:44.370 --> 00:43:47.790
equipment or the video
equipment that I need.

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:50.940
Really being cognizant
of all those equipment

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:53.880
costs that maybe you haven't
incurred since you started

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:56.490
the venture, but
these are real things

00:43:56.490 --> 00:44:00.460
that you need every
day to do that.

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:01.630
Excellent.

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:03.100
I think maybe one or two more.

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:05.320
I don't want to overstay
our welcome or anything.

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:07.300
You're not overstaying.

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:11.000
So how does one sort of
anticipate creating balance.

00:44:11.000 --> 00:44:13.030
How do you safely
assess risk when

00:44:13.030 --> 00:44:14.770
you're attempting
to forecast things

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:17.350
like revenue versus
expenses, especially

00:44:17.350 --> 00:44:20.710
in a startup that has
few, if any, guarantees

00:44:20.710 --> 00:44:22.960
of upfront financing?

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:23.860
Yeah.

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:27.100
So I think as we've
seen in the last year,

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:31.540
even the best plans can go,
so one thing I will say,

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:34.900
during COVID, I've
spoken with funders

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:39.490
of all kinds of
different industries,

00:44:39.490 --> 00:44:42.370
and the common theme
has been, we're

00:44:42.370 --> 00:44:46.473
no longer looking at performance
for the last year, right?

00:44:46.473 --> 00:44:47.890
The only thing
that really matters

00:44:47.890 --> 00:44:49.973
is what companies have
done in the last six months

00:44:49.973 --> 00:44:51.520
to three months, right?

00:44:51.520 --> 00:44:55.300
And the ability to pivot or
to use whatever resources

00:44:55.300 --> 00:44:59.620
are available to solve a
problem and move forward

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:01.600
has become much more
important in terms

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:06.530
of making a decision about whom
or what to back or to fund.

00:45:06.530 --> 00:45:09.610
So I think to that point we're
in a chaotic environment.

00:45:09.610 --> 00:45:16.870
I think understanding
what expenses and factors

00:45:16.870 --> 00:45:19.840
you might be able
to control, and what

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.580
might be beyond your
control, and just

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:25.210
being very upfront about
that, and having backup plans.

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:27.700
I mean you can do
sensitivity analyses, right?

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:29.218
So I've worked in
non-profits where

00:45:29.218 --> 00:45:31.510
we had to do this because we
didn't know what was going

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:34.270
to happen to a currency
or an interest rate

00:45:34.270 --> 00:45:35.860
in the local market.

00:45:35.860 --> 00:45:37.630
And so you can
actually do those.

00:45:37.630 --> 00:45:39.980
Excel is a wonderful tool.

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:43.630
It's not wonderful, it's
not always wonderful.

00:45:43.630 --> 00:45:45.730
But it has the
ability to allow you

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:48.490
to copy your spreadsheets over.

00:45:48.490 --> 00:45:52.750
You can do those forecasts,
and you can say, hey,

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:56.350
nobody can come
to our performance

00:45:56.350 --> 00:46:00.038
because there's a
pandemic, but look

00:46:00.038 --> 00:46:01.330
at what other people are doing.

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:03.230
Are there other
performance models

00:46:03.230 --> 00:46:04.480
and what are our alternatives?

00:46:04.480 --> 00:46:08.340
And being able to say, OK,
under these circumstances,

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:11.490
we think we can
still make this work.

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:13.848
Perfect.

00:46:13.848 --> 00:46:15.140
That's a great piece of advice.

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:18.170
And building on that, thinking
about this competition

00:46:18.170 --> 00:46:21.560
more broadly, if you could
think back on the business

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:23.300
that you've seen
and participated in,

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:27.682
if you have one pearl
of wisdom or takeaway

00:46:27.682 --> 00:46:30.140
for students competing in this
competition, what would that

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:32.340
be?

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:34.070
I would say, really
focus on your story

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:36.110
and why you are the
right person to lead

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:37.250
this venture at this time.

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:43.460
I think anyone wants
to feel like you're

00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:46.790
going to do this
regardless of where you

00:46:46.790 --> 00:46:49.940
land in the competition, right?

00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:53.100
That just underscored exactly
what we're trying to do here.

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:54.980
We're trying to be
good storytellers.

00:46:54.980 --> 00:46:59.000
And, you know, we're trying
not to be so end-game driven.

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:02.060
It's really great if you walk
away with the great big check,

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:05.210
but it's also pretty great
if you learn anything

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:09.110
from these workshops, from the
mentorship, from the feedback.

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:13.082
So ideally everybody tells a
better story when they come out

00:47:13.082 --> 00:47:14.040
than when they came in.

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:15.660
So thank you for that.

00:47:15.660 --> 00:47:16.160
So, yeah.

00:47:16.160 --> 00:47:19.550
So it's really about telling
the story of the world you're

00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:22.640
trying to build, and
then having people want

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:25.160
to join you on your journey.

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:26.150
Perfect.

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:27.540
This has been fantastic.

00:47:27.540 --> 00:47:30.230
I really appreciate your
time and the insights

00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:32.500
and thank you so much.

